# deploy/Caddyfile
#
# Puts HTTPS in front of the bot on a plain VM (e.g. an Oracle Always-Free
# instance). Meta's WhatsApp Cloud API requires a public HTTPS webhook with a
# valid CA-signed certificate — a raw IP or self-signed cert is rejected.
# Caddy obtains and renews a free Let's Encrypt certificate automatically, then
# reverse-proxies to the uvicorn app (the systemd unit in this folder) on
# localhost:8000.
#
# Prerequisites:
#   * A domain/subdomain whose A record points to this VM's public IP.
#     A free DuckDNS subdomain (https://www.duckdns.org) works fine.
#   * Ports 80 AND 443 open — in BOTH the Oracle VCN security list/NSG and the
#     instance's own firewall (Oracle Ubuntu images block these by default):
#         sudo iptables -I INPUT 6 -p tcp --dport 80  -j ACCEPT
#         sudo iptables -I INPUT 6 -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT
#         sudo netfilter-persistent save
#
# Install Caddy (Ubuntu): https://caddyserver.com/docs/install#debian-ubuntu-raspbian
# Then replace the placeholder below, put this file at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile,
# and: sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# Your Meta webhook Callback URL is then:
#     https://your-subdomain.duckdns.org/meta/webhook

your-subdomain.duckdns.org {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}
